      "writtenOn": "2025-02-13T13:01:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I started reviewing this, got concerned about the cost of unordered_set lookups everywhere and then got confused as to why we need this.\n\nHow can the fuzzer produce recursive chains? The wire format doesn\u0027t support doing that IIRC because each member of the chain is written one after the other. The chaining is implicit so loops shouldn\u0027t happen.",
      "revId": "8fc2b0b5abb7b71c34fdf78e3d85542a1e3b8a90",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9f096b66_ce06a81a",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-02-13T22:55:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I won\u0027t get to updating this today w.r.t the other comments, but, tldr; it\u0027s not exactly a \"recursive\" chain, it\u0027s just a REALLY long chain that is possible because `PipelineLayoutStorageAttachment` becoming extensible is the first time we have an extension struct (`PipelineLayoutPixelLocalStorage`), that contains an extensible member. So even though it\u0027s not actually valid, you can keep chaining these together to create extremely long chains.\n\nIf you look at the bug, (and the fuzzer bug that is also linked in it), the fuzzer essentially produces a chain that is greater than 256 chains long. We end up stack overflowing because our deserialization code recurses to handle chained structs.\n\nThe fuzzer only caught the deserialization error, but in practice, it\u0027s equally possible to cause the stack overflow on serialization side by creating and serializing a really long chain.",
      "parentUuid": "e04d2ccf_70e2f38b",
      "revId": "8fc2b0b5abb7b71c34fdf78e3d85542a1e3b8a90",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8e0e60bb_490b9f2f",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000002
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-02-17T14:49:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ah thanks, that makes sense, and we can\u0027t really make the recursion into a loop because we need to deserialize the rest of the extensible structure as well.\n\nHowever the problem seems to be caused by the fact that the support for chained structs in the wire can handle any chained struct in any extension chain. However we now have info about what can be chained where with the \"chain roots\", and can force a validation error on the server when the client sees an unexpected sType (with `DawnWireInvalidExtension`). So WDYT of making wire code for each extension struct handle only what we know it is a chained struct for? I don\u0027t see a case today or in the future where this more precise recursion could stack overflow.\n\n(only maybe on the client if in the future we add S-\u003eC WGSL reflection maybe?)",
      "parentUuid": "9f096b66_ce06a81a",
      "revId": "8fc2b0b5abb7b71c34fdf78e3d85542a1e3b8a90",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "014cb6a1_97c03bf7",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-02-18T19:32:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yup that is one option that Kai and I discussed. The downside of that is that it requires us duplicating the validation logic in the client serialization, the server deserialization, and the actual Dawn frontend. The idea here was to defer the validation error to be generated by the Dawn frontend logic, and simplify the wire transition as much as possible.\n\nEffectively, we are still checking if the types are valid in this change, but instead of injecting the error directly, we just forward it along so that the Dawn frontend will eventually generate the error. I think the general idea here is to make the wire do as little as possible so that we don\u0027t need to maintain validation logic in multiple places. (IIUC, adding logic to make the validation error look nice with context will be the annoying part about injecting from the wire.)",
      "parentUuid": "8e0e60bb_490b9f2f",
      "revId": "8fc2b0b5abb7b71c34fdf78e3d85542a1e3b8a90",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6493a3f8_a236f192",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000002
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-02-19T14:11:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We can discuss it later today, but don\u0027t we need to have the validation in both the `wire::server` and `native` already? Given that the validation will be automatically updated with `dawn.json` changes, it doesn\u0027t seem like a huge cost to check for things that we don\u0027t know about in `wire::client` as well. Plus this CL already does that.\u003d\n\nI\u0027m not sure I got the part about the \"context\" for the errors: these would be API usage errors basically, so just saying \"we cannot support this structure in one of the chains\" should be sufficient. We could try to print labels etc but the marginal value seems extremely tiny.",
      "parentUuid": "014cb6a1_97c03bf7",
      "revId": "8fc2b0b5abb7b71c34fdf78e3d85542a1e3b8a90",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "769b4dbb_40d42bdd",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
